                eng: "Making a personal change is exciting, but building a new habit can be very difficult. Many people want to improve their lives. They hope to learn a new language, start a diet, or read more books. However, they often give up after only a few weeks. Why does this happen?\n\nFirst, people often try to change too many things at once. For example, a person might want to wake up early, run five kilometers, and eat a perfect breakfast all on the same day. This requires a lot of effort and energy. When people feel tired, they like going back to their old, comfortable routines. To succeed, experts suggest starting very small. If you want to read more, do not try to read a whole book in one week. Instead, you should aim to read just one page a day. This small step is easy to keep doing over time.\n\nSecond, motivation is not always enough. You need to connect your new habit to an existing routine. For instance, if you want to drink more water, you can promise to drink a glass immediately after you brush your teeth. Brushing your teeth becomes the automatic \"trigger\" for the new healthy habit.\n\nFinally, it is important to remember why you want to change. If you love to travel, learning English will make your future trips much more enjoyable. Reminding yourself of your big goal will help you stay focused on the small, daily steps. It takes time to develop a skill, but the result is always worth the effort.",
